1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,060
They're engineered with the utmost seriousness and manufactured with unprecedented precision.

2
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:08,660
All in the pursuit of play.

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:16,880
But whether hit, rolled, slammed, or driven to extremes, they always bounce back for more.

4
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:20,600
Now, Balls on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:30,700
Balls.

6
00:00:30,701 --> 00:00:39,720
They spin through our lives in predictable ways, so much so that we take their performance

7
00:00:39,721 --> 00:00:40,965
for granted.

8
00:00:40,966 --> 00:00:49,160
But the fuzzy, pebbled, gleaming, dimpled surfaces on most modern balls conceal

9
00:00:49,161 --> 00:00:53,579
an impressive history of research and development.

10
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:57,380
One ball, however, is a bit of a throwback.

11
00:00:57,381 --> 00:01:02,460
Befitting its smash-mouth sport, the football is forged from the basics.

12
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,680
Cowhide, sweat, and perseverance.

13
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:16,540
At Wilson's Ada, Ohio plant, most of the employees are veterans who have been on the job for at least two decades.

14
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:22,660
Cutting, stitching, turning, and lacing footballs for the National Football League.

15
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,080
But they make more than just NFL balls.

16
00:01:26,081 --> 00:01:30,420
We make them for colleges, high schools, we make them for the youth league, whether it

17
00:01:30,421 --> 00:01:33,680
would be midget, tiwi, or the junior high.

18
00:01:33,681 --> 00:01:38,260
We make between 3,000 and 5,000 balls here every day, Monday through Thursday.

19
00:01:38,261 --> 00:01:43,260
Somewhere around 800,000 balls will be made this year.

20
00:01:43,261 --> 00:01:48,200
The plant will produce about 30,000 balls for the NFL each year, a little less than

21
00:01:48,201 --> 00:01:51,460
1,000 for each of the 32 teams.

22
00:01:51,461 --> 00:01:59,140
A pro ball begins with cowhide that has been dyed, pressed into a pebbled grain for gripping,

23
00:01:59,141 --> 00:02:04,640
and treated with a resin-based material to give it a tacky texture.

24
00:02:04,641 --> 00:02:08,900
Still, quarterbacks feel the need to rough up a new ball.

25
00:02:08,901 --> 00:02:14,980
When you get a football and it's brand new, it comes with a kind of a slick film.

26
00:02:14,981 --> 00:02:18,600
You just want to take it and throw it into the ground a couple times and just rub it

27
00:02:18,601 --> 00:02:21,840
in with a little water and make a nice tack on the ball.

28
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:26,820
A football starts its life in pieces.

29
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:31,140
Each hide provides enough leather for 13 to 14 balls.

30
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,200
All the balls we make here have to weigh a particular weight.

31
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:37,860
For example, the NFL weighs between 14 and 15 ounces.

32
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:44,040
An NFL ball that's too light or too heavy will end up in a store, not a pro game.

33
00:02:44,041 --> 00:02:51,040
The Wilson and NFL logos are heat stamped at 400 degrees Fahrenheit from foil decals.

34
00:02:51,041 --> 00:02:58,040
Next, plasticized cloth linings are machine-stitched to the panels.

35
00:02:58,041 --> 00:03:03,040
The lining is what gives a ball a strength and shape. It's not the leather.

36
00:03:03,041 --> 00:03:07,040
The leather can shrink or grow depending on the kind of weather we're having outside.

37
00:03:07,041 --> 00:03:13,040
The balls are stitched inside out so that the stitching won't show.

38
00:03:13,041 --> 00:03:13,741
show.

39
00:03:13,742 --> 00:03:19,280
They're then turned right side out by a ball turner who will turn between 500 and 600 balls in a day.

40
00:03:20,440 --> 00:03:23,200
The reason we put it in a steam box is to give the leather more

41
00:03:23,201 --> 00:03:27,658
applied facilities where it's softer and a little easier for us to turn.

42
00:03:27,659 --> 00:03:30,320
But you've got to use a lot of body weight.

43
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:33,950
Your body can do the work in the car.

44
00:03:33,951 --> 00:03:38,080
It's hard on the muscles, hard on the arms, shoulders.

45
00:03:38,081 --> 00:03:43,960
but at the end it's all the same.

46
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:48,980
Next a polyurethane bladder is inserted.

47
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:53,040
The air hole is pulled through, snipped

48
00:03:53,041 --> 00:03:55,200
and the bladder is partially inflated.

49
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:01,340
A lacer then laces the ball.

50
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:04,260
They work at a pace of 300 balls a day.

51
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:12,080
The laces function as a grip area for a quarterback.

52
00:04:12,081 --> 00:04:17,960
You want to anchor one finger on the laces so that you can torque the ball and spin it

53
00:04:17,961 --> 00:04:19,760
out when you throw it.

54
00:04:19,761 --> 00:04:24,220
The main thing is to hold the ball in your fingers and in the thumb.

55
00:04:24,221 --> 00:04:27,640
Not too tight, not too loose, but just a firm grip.

56
00:04:27,641 --> 00:04:31,420
That becomes important when you throw the spiral because you want the fingers to leave

57
00:04:31,421 --> 00:04:33,420
the ball consecutively.

58
00:04:33,421 --> 00:04:39,360
first, ring finger, middle finger, and then pointer finger is the one that torques and

59
00:04:39,361 --> 00:04:41,420
spins the ball and makes the spiral.

60
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:48,600
The spinning causes a gyroscopic effect, preventing the ball from tumbling or wobbling, which

61
00:04:48,601 --> 00:04:50,120
would increase aerodynamic drag.

62
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:54,380
The spiral stabilizes the ball in the orientation with the least air resistance.

63
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:02,420
Football, a game which evolved from soccer and rugby at American colleges in the 19th

64
00:05:02,421 --> 00:05:03,121
century.

65
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:06,180
originally used an unwieldy oval ball.

66
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:11,220
The game didn't even allow the forward pass until 1906.

67
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:18,440
In 1935, with the advent of a smaller pointed ball,

68
00:05:18,441 --> 00:05:20,300
the modern passing game was born.

69
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:27,060
At the Wilson plant,

70
00:05:27,061 --> 00:05:29,480
the ball gets a final forming under pressure.

71
00:05:29,481 --> 00:05:31,460
Each ball starts with about five pounds of pressure

72
00:05:31,461 --> 00:05:32,860
when we put it in this little cavity,

73
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:35,840
and we're going to put in about 100 pounds of air in the ball

74
00:05:35,841 --> 00:05:39,080
to conform it to that cavity to smooth out the seams of the ball.

75
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:44,420
Finally, at 13 pounds of pressure,

76
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:48,240
the most perfect balls are ready to be used in professional football games.

77
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:52,800
This idea that balls should have a uniform shape,

78
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:55,620
or even that games should have consistent rules,

79
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:57,120
is a modern concept.

80
00:05:57,820 --> 00:06:01,100
In antiquity, balls were used for rituals.

81
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:09,540
And they were mostly rituals of fertility that signified the rites of passage between the winter season and the summer season.

82
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:18,520
The Mesoamerican game of Poké Talk that flourished more than a thousand years ago was a team game played with a solid heavy rubber ball.

83
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:28,580
For the Mayans, the game may have had many meanings, as ritual reenactments of wars or as a way to play out seasonal struggles with the ball representing the sun.

84
00:06:28,581 --> 00:06:34,300
Poké talk games would sometimes end in a sacrifice from the losing team.

85
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:38,740
The sacrifice was the decapitation of the captain,

86
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,560
which would now release the head from the body,

87
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:45,180
and you would have the ball.

88
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:50,120
And sometimes that skull would be the center of the ball,

89
00:06:50,121 --> 00:06:52,400
and then the ball would be built around it.

90
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,020
By the Middle Ages in Europe,

91
00:06:57,021 --> 00:07:01,300
some balls were being made from inflated animal bladders covered in hides.

92
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:05,660
They were used in a violent territorial game known as camp ball,

93
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:09,360
and the word camp, an Anglo-Saxon term for battle.

94
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:13,440
The ball had to be moved into the opponent's end of town.

95
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:15,480
Whole neighborhoods battled.

96
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:21,460
The game lasted entire days, involved hundreds of men,

97
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:26,840
and featured hand-to-hand combat that often resulted in serious injury or death.

98
00:07:27,020 --> 00:07:32,020
Over time, this destructive game was moved out of towns onto fields.

99
00:07:32,021 --> 00:07:37,020
And by the 17th century in England, it evolved into soccer and rugby.

100
00:07:37,021 --> 00:07:42,020
Once these games crossed the Atlantic to the United States in the 19th century,

101
00:07:42,021 --> 00:07:44,020
they evolved into American football.

102
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:52,020
But it's soccer, with its European origins, that has remained the world's most popular game.

103
00:07:52,021 --> 00:07:54,020
Move in a hurry!

104
00:07:57,020 --> 00:08:03,920
The earliest soccer balls were irregular in shape because they depended on the size and shape of the pig's bladder from which they were made.

105
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:09,660
Leather coverings were introduced later to help the ball keep its shape.

106
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:13,580
The field wasn't a flat field either.

107
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:19,560
The surface wasn't anywhere like the surfaces today so that the ball would bounce crazily.

108
00:08:19,561 --> 00:08:25,480
Because the ball wasn't round wasn't a problem because the surface wouldn't let a good ball bounce correctly.

109
00:08:27,020 --> 00:08:29,360
Sunker began to roll toward uniformity

110
00:08:29,361 --> 00:08:31,920
with Charles Goodyear's discovery of the vulcanization

111
00:08:31,921 --> 00:08:33,620
of rubber in 1839.

112
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:39,400
Vulcanization is a heat and chemical process

113
00:08:39,401 --> 00:08:44,680
that takes a naturally occurring tree sap rubber

114
00:08:44,681 --> 00:08:49,660
and gives it a firmer, moldable consistency

115
00:08:49,661 --> 00:08:52,480
that actually allows it to form into a shape

116
00:08:52,481 --> 00:08:53,380
and hold that shape.

117
00:08:53,381 --> 00:09:02,740
In 1855, Charles Goodyear designed and built the first soccer ball made of vulcanized rubber panels glued at the seams.

118
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:12,260
In 1862, Richard Linden, a shoemaker from rugby England, designed the first inflatable rubber bladder,

119
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:20,000
and the basics of the modern uniform mass-produced inflatable ball were born, like this ball from the 1930s.

120
00:09:20,001 --> 00:09:25,080
We have a bladder, and you can see the neck that was on it.

121
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:26,440
The neck sticks out.

122
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:30,780
This is why not only soccer balls were laced in this day,

123
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:32,880
but why a football was laced.

124
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:38,280
They had this neck that had to be pulled out, attached to the pump,

125
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:42,360
the air blown in, and then they'd take a string or something

126
00:09:42,361 --> 00:09:44,720
and crush this to hold the air in,

127
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:49,400
tuck it back underneath the leather, and lace the ball back up.

128
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:56,260
Once soccer began to appear regularly on black and white televisions in the mid-1960s,

129
00:09:56,500 --> 00:10:02,120
something had to be done about the tan ball that appeared gray and got lost against the background.

130
00:10:02,980 --> 00:10:11,220
That's when this ball's developed, the black and white ball made of pentagons and hexagons,

131
00:10:11,460 --> 00:10:14,340
which is now very visible on TV.

132
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:19,240
Adidas designed the black and white ball for the 1970 World Cup.

133
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:28,740
The design, 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons, echoed Buckminster Fuller's geodesic domes, which were becoming popular at the time.

134
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:39,180
Still, this ball made from leather, which gets heavy when wet, is a naturally irregular product, and it requires stitching that can stretch unevenly.

135
00:10:39,181 --> 00:10:50,660
Adidas' 21st century World Cup ball overcomes these issues by trading leather for waterproof polyurethane panels that are thermally fused together.

136
00:10:52,340 --> 00:11:01,300
Unlike previous balls where the panels were flat and drawn together into a sphere by the stitching process,

137
00:11:01,301 --> 00:11:14,440
These panels are formed in a curved form so that before the panels are put together, they're already in the curvature they need to be to make a round ball.

138
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:20,360
Therefore, it will more likely do what the kicker intended.

139
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:22,833
I like these new balls.

140
00:11:22,834 --> 00:11:26,660
They're kind of neat, but it's still about the players in the end.

141
00:11:26,661 --> 00:11:32,940
Though players dominate sports, balls often get a surprising amount of scrutiny.

142
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:38,100
None more than the ball that is increasingly hit out of the park in America's game.

143
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:40,060
Is it juiced?

144
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:41,580
Wound too tight?

145
00:11:42,440 --> 00:11:45,980
Inside, it's got a story ready to be unraveled.

146
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:53,360
The home team must provide the referee with 24 footballs for each National Football League game.

147
00:11:53,361 --> 00:11:55,880
This Modern Marvels fact is brought to you...

148
00:11:55,881 --> 00:11:56,880
to sing a song.

149
00:11:56,881 --> 00:11:59,380
Return to Balls on Modern Marbles.

150
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:07,760
Baseball.

151
00:12:07,761 --> 00:12:15,700
It's a game of statistics, precision, and most of all,

152
00:12:15,701 --> 00:12:17,800
tradition.

153
00:12:17,801 --> 00:12:19,900
But the ball in play in the major leagues

154
00:12:19,901 --> 00:12:23,240
of this most American of games is not made in America.

155
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:29,020
Though they're made of U.S. supplied materials,

156
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:33,560
Major League balls are manufactured only at Rawlings Baseball Plant,

157
00:12:34,140 --> 00:12:37,560
in a country where soccer is king, Costa Rica.

158
00:12:39,100 --> 00:12:43,820
While the game of baseball may be lost in translation in this Central American nation,

159
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:47,120
some of its spirit is alive and well at Rawlings,

160
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:51,000
where the ball sewers' weeks are measured out in baseballs.

161
00:12:52,340 --> 00:12:55,560
They have an incentive that if they meet their quantity,

162
00:12:55,561 --> 00:12:59,580
they get to go home as soon as they meet it and they get paid the full week and

163
00:12:59,581 --> 00:13:05,240
they have also the option to stay and get extra if they continue sewing up to

164
00:13:05,241 --> 00:13:09,180
a 30% of their salary extra.

165
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:15,120
A ball sewer sinks the first stitches deeper into the ball to anchor them. The

166
00:13:15,121 --> 00:13:19,840
sowers use a two-handed motion for greater efficiency.

167
00:13:19,841 --> 00:13:27,440
You use two needles, then you thread them and then you wax them so you can make a ball faster.

168
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:32,800
Then you put them in the center with the stool and you pull the needles and you make the ball.

169
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:37,580
Rocio Gamboa is one of the fastest ball sewers in the plant.

170
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:40,380
Her secret? Staying competitive.

171
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:46,620
Add the clock to keep track of time, making it a competition.

172
00:13:46,621 --> 00:13:50,180
The fun thing is to try to take less time with each ball.

173
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:53,220
It's a type of a game. I have fun with that.

174
00:13:56,960 --> 00:13:59,760
Though many companies, including Rawlings, have tried,

175
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:03,140
none have succeeded in automating the ball sewing process.

176
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:07,640
It takes 11 weeks to train a sewer to make even uniform stitches.

177
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:14,080
And as any baseball pitcher will tell you, those stitches, or seams, are the key.

178
00:14:14,081 --> 00:14:21,220
basic curveball you want to do you can grab it middle finger up against the seam create the

179
00:14:21,221 --> 00:14:25,240
friction against the seam all you do is cock your wrist in like that and come right over the top of

180
00:14:25,241 --> 00:14:31,420
the ball spinning it off that seam to create a downer rotation and physics takes over and the

181
00:14:31,421 --> 00:14:38,680
spin will make the ball drop the top spin forces the top half of the ball into air resistance while

182
00:14:38,681 --> 00:14:41,200
The bottom half of the ball is spinning away from that resistance.

183
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:44,700
Air pressure above the ball is greater than below,

184
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:49,240
so the ball curves downward as much as a foot or more as it travels to the plate.

185
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:51,420
It's all about arm angle.

186
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:54,160
12 to 6, you're going to be throwing it right here straight down through the ball.

187
00:14:54,340 --> 00:14:56,620
And it's very rare to have a true 12 to 6 curveball.

188
00:14:56,900 --> 00:14:58,940
A lot of people throw from a little lower 3 quarter angle,

189
00:14:59,060 --> 00:15:01,100
so you're going to get more of a sweeping effect on the ball.

190
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:07,460
All professional baseballs have to fall within the major league specifications

191
00:15:07,461 --> 00:15:11,140
to weigh between 5 and 5.25 ounces

192
00:15:11,141 --> 00:15:15,660
and to measure between 9 and 9.25 inches in circumference.

193
00:15:16,620 --> 00:15:19,200
These specs have been in place since 1872.

194
00:15:20,700 --> 00:15:23,600
From the inside out, the modern ball's components are

195
00:15:23,601 --> 00:15:27,680
a center pill of cork surrounded by two layers of rubber,

196
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:31,300
three yarn windings, one cotton thread winding,

197
00:15:31,301 --> 00:15:36,020
a latex adhesive, a cowhide cover, and red thread stitching.

198
00:15:37,460 --> 00:15:41,860
In the early days of baseball, not much about the game was consistent.

199
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:44,660
Neither the ball nor the rules.

200
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:50,380
Clubs bought handmade balls with rubber cores, yarn windings, and horsehide covers

201
00:15:50,381 --> 00:15:53,720
from cobblers or tanneries, or made their own.

202
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:59,280
This is a handmade baseball, circa 1860s.

203
00:15:59,940 --> 00:16:03,600
It has the typical, what we call now, lemon peel seam.

204
00:16:03,601 --> 00:16:07,920
So you have a single piece of leather here that's seamed up to a point at the top.

205
00:16:09,260 --> 00:16:12,740
By the mid-1870s with the formation of the National League,

206
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:16,000
a gentleman named A.G. Spaulding, who was a star baseball player,

207
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:19,860
quit baseball at the peak of his career, to move into the manufacturing field.

208
00:16:20,020 --> 00:16:23,240
And he founded the Spaulding Sporting Goods conglomerate that still exists today.

209
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:31,700
One of his first real coups was to be awarded the official baseball of the National League.

210
00:16:31,701 --> 00:16:33,720
And he actually was not paying him.

211
00:16:33,820 --> 00:16:35,420
He was paying for the rights to brag.

212
00:16:36,040 --> 00:16:38,820
And then he could sell his baseball to lots of other people.

213
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:42,240
Early balls had a rubber center.

214
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:45,760
In 1910, this was replaced by a lighter cork core.

215
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:49,400
To compensate for the weight change,

216
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:52,400
additional yarn was wrapped more tightly around the core.

217
00:16:52,780 --> 00:16:54,340
And the ball became livelier.

218
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:59,800
By the mid-1920s, that cork was protected with a double layer of rubber.

219
00:16:59,801 --> 00:17:04,500
That was introduced in the 1925 season, used in the 25 World Series,

220
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:07,140
and then throughout the 26th season,

221
00:17:07,380 --> 00:17:10,580
and actually, essentially, that's the same type of baseball that's used today.

222
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:16,420
Rowlings took over the Major League Baseball contract from Spaulding in 1977.

223
00:17:17,900 --> 00:17:21,720
We're producing 2.4 million baseballs a year.

224
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:26,640
That's about 50,000 balls a week, 10,000 a day.

225
00:17:26,641 --> 00:17:32,880
Most of the ball is made up of three yarn windings and one cotton thread winding.

226
00:17:33,880 --> 00:17:36,360
Rawlings keeps the automated winding process secret

227
00:17:36,361 --> 00:17:39,720
because they've built machines specifically for Major League Baseball.

228
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:46,100
What's not off-limits is the outside of the ball.

229
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:51,440
Since 1974, that covering has been made of cowhide, not horsehide,

230
